{
  "slug": "artist:the-roots",
  "name": "The Roots",
  "editorial_article": "Formed in 1987 at the Philadelphia High School for the Creative and Performing Arts, The Roots evolved from the street-corner busking duo of drummer Ahmir \"Questlove\" Thompson and rapper Tariq \"Black Thought\" Trotter into a critically acclaimed, live-instrumentation hip-hop band. Their debut independent album, Organix (1993), was recorded following a short stint in London, but it was the 1996 release Illadelph Halflife that established their critical standing, featuring the hit \"What They Do.\" The group helped pioneer the neo-soul sound through their membership in the collective Soulquarians, collaborating with D'Angelo, Common, and Erykah Badu. Their 1999 breakthrough album, Things Fall Apart, achieved platinum certification and included the Grammy-winning single \"You Got Me.\" Known for their long-standing role as the house band for Jimmy Fallon, first on Late Night and subsequently on The Tonight Show, the ensemble has maintained an eclectic approach to music that incorporates jazz, alternative rap, and experimental production.",
